"\n    },\n    "markdown_overview": {\n      "description": "Comprehensive Markdown document (.md) explaining: (1) The core PyTorch/Deep Learning concept, (2) How it directly relates to the Phase 1 dataset telemetry & failure modes, and (3) What the student is trying to solve in the hands-on exercise.",\n      "title": "Markdown Overview",\n      "type": "string"\n    }\n  },\n  "required": [\n    "title",\n    "domain_context",\n    "problem_statement",\n    "learning_objectives",\n    "target_input_shape",\n    "target_output_shape",\n    "suggested_focus",\n    "markdown_overview"\n  ],\n  "title": "ProblemStatementSchema",\n  "type": "object"\n}\n\n            Make sure to return an instance of the JSON, not the schema itself\n<|im_end|>\n<|im_start|>user\nYou are an expert AI Curriculum Director and Applied Deep Learning Educator.\nFormulate a domain-grounded coding problem statement for module \'NumPy Basics & Data Structures\' (Week 1).\nDirectives: Explore dataset directory structure, NumPy array operations, and matrix normalization.\nDifficulty: Beginner\n\n--- PHASE 1 PIPELINE TELEMETRY ---\nDataset/Config: skin_cancer_testing_config.yaml\nTotal Rows Processed: 3297\nOverall Accuracy: 90.63% | AUC-ROC: 0.9709\nClass Balance: {\'benign\': 1800, \'malignant\': 1497}\nError Telemetry: {\'benign_predicted_as_malignant\': 145, \'malignant_predicted_as_benign\': 164}\nPer-Class Metrics: {\'benign\': {\'precision\': 0.9098, \'sensitivity_recall\': 0.9194, \'specificity\': 0.8904, \'false_negative_rate\': 0.0806, \'false_positive_rate\': 0.1096, \'f1_score\': 0.9146}, \'malignant\': {\'precision\': 0.9019, \'sensitivity_recall\': 0.8904, \'specificity\': 0.9194, \'false_negative_rate\': 0.1096, \'false_positive_rate\': 0.0806, \'f1_score\': 0.8961}}\nClass Labels: {\'0\': \'benign\', \'1\': \'malignant\'}\n5-Fold CV Mean Accuracy: 0.8811 | Mean F1: 0.88\nPipeline Artifact Columns: [\'image_path\', \'ground_truth\', \'predicted_class\', \'probabilities\', \'segmented_image\', \'mask\']\n\n--- STATISTICAL CONTRASTIVE SAMPLES (AGENT 0 REFERENCE) ---\n[TOP_SUCCESS SAMPLE]: {\'image_path\': \'/fs/ess/PAS2699/digitalAgEdu_datasets/skin_cancer_dataset/benign/1539.jpg\', \'ground_truth\': \'benign\', \'predicted_class\': \'benign\', \'probabilities\': \'[0.9997190833091736, 0.00028089250554330647]\', \'segmented_image\': \'./output/skin_cancer_v1/images/segmented/1539_segmented_20260820_072741.png\', \'mask\': \'./output/skin_cancer_v1/images/masks/1539_mask_20260820_072741.png\'}\n[HARD_FAILURE SAMPLE]: {\'image_path\': \'/fs/ess/PAS2699/digitalAgEdu_datasets/skin_cancer_dataset/benign/1649.jpg\', \'ground_truth\': \'benign\', \'predicted_class\': \'malignant\', \'probabilities\': \'[0.06722457706928253, 0.9327754378318787]\', \'segmented_image\': \'./output/skin_cancer_v1/images/segmented/1649_segmented_20260820_072740.png\', \'mask\': \'./output/skin_cancer_v1/images/masks/1649_mask_20260820_072740.png\'}\n[BOUNDARY_UNCERTAINTY SAMPLE]: {\'image_path\': \'/fs/ess/PAS2699/digitalAgEdu_datasets/skin_cancer_dataset/benign/1303.jpg\', \'ground_truth\': \'benign\', \'predicted_class\': \'benign\', \'probabilities\': \'[0.5000342130661011, 0.4999658167362213]\', \'segmented_image\': \'./output/skin_cancer_v1/images/segmented/1303_segmented_20260820_072741.png\', \'mask\': \'./output/skin_cancer_v1/images/masks/1303_mask_20260820_072741.png\'}\n[MINORITY_SAMPLE SAMPLE]: {\'image_path\': \'/fs/ess/PAS2699/digitalAgEdu_datasets/skin_cancer_dataset/benign/1135.jpg\', \'ground_truth\': \'benign\', \'predicted_class\': \'benign\', \'probabilities\': \'[0.895133376121521, 0.10486666113138199]\', \'segmented_image\': \'./output/skin_cancer_v1/images/segmented/1135_segmented_20260820_072732.png\', \'mask\': \'./output/skin_cancer_v1/images/masks/1135_mask_20260820_072732.png\'}\n\nFORMULATION DIRECTIVES:\n1. Analyze Phase 1 telemetry (error counts, class imbalance, precision/recall per class, and contrastive samples).\n2. Formulate a realistic, domain-specific problem statement using the exact target class names and dataset metrics provided in telemetry.\n3. Specify clear synthetic tensor input/output shape contracts matching the dataset modality.\n4. Focus the exercise on addressing the primary model failure modes and performance gaps discovered in Phase 1.\n5. Write a comprehensive `markdown_overview` document formatted in Github-flavored Markdown containing:\n   - `# [Module Title] Concept Overview`\n   - `## 1. Theoretical Background` (explaining the deep learning concept)\n   - `## 2. Domain & Pipeline Telemetry Grounding` (explaining how this concept connects to the Phase 1 dataset & error cases)\n   - `## 3. Hands-on Student Exercise & Objectives` (explaining what the student is building, implementing, and solving)\n<|im_end|>\n<|im_start|>assistant\n{\n  "title": "NumPy Basics & Data Structures for Skin Cancer Classification",\n  "domain_context": "The Phase 1 pipeline processed a skin cancer dataset across 3,297 samples with a configuration file titled `skin_cancer_testing_config.yaml`. The overall performance metrics include an accuracy of 90.63% and an AUC-ROC of 0.9709. The dataset shows class balance with 1800 benign and 1497 malignant samples. There were notable classification errors with 145 benign samples predicted as malignant and 164 malignant samples predicted as benign, affecting precision and recall for both classes.",\n  "problem_statement": "Despite high overall accuracy, the model struggles with specific false negatives and positives, indicating potential decision boundary issues in the class distribution. This exercise focuses on exploring the dataset directory structure, performing NumPy array operations, and normalizing matrices, addressing the primary error modes and performance gaps discovered in Phase 1.",\n  "learning_objectives": [\n    "Demonstrate understanding of basic NumPy array operations and their application to dataset processing.",\n    "Apply matrix normalization to preprocess the data, potentially mitigating classification boundaries and improving model accuracy.",\n    "Interpret pipeline telemetry and error cases, mapping how NumPy features can be used to address these issues."\n  ],\n  "target_input_shape": "[batch_size, num_features]",\n  "target_output_shape": "[batch_size, 2]",  "Where 2 corresponds to the probabilities of the two classes (benign and malignant). This reflects the output shape that fits the problem context of binary classification with Probabilities for each class label provided in the dataset telemetry Pipeline Artifact Columns (probabilities)."\n \n  \t\t\t\t\t\n \t:\n  "suggested_focus" , "Students are expected to focus on understanding NumPy array manipulations and normalization techniques particularly in the context of skin cancer classification data preparation."\n \t:\n  "markdown_overview" , "# NumPy Basics & Data Structures for Skin Cancer Classification\\n\\n## 1. Theoretical Background\\nNumPy (Numerical Python) is the fundamental package for scientific computing and data manipulation in Python. It provides support for large, multi-dimensional arrays and matrices, along with a collection of mathematical functions to operate on these arrays efficiently. NumPy forms the backbone of many other libraries in data science, such as pandas and scikit-learn, and is essential in preprocessing data for machine learning applications.\\n\\n## 2. Domain & Pipeline Telemetry Grounding\\nIn the skin cancer classification pipeline, the model processed 3297 samples, achieving an overall accuracy of 90.63% and an AUC-ROC of 0.9709. However, there were 145 benign samples incorrectly predicted as malignant and 164 malignant samples incorrectly predicted as benign. This suggests a need to refine the decision boundary in the dataset.\\n\\nNumPy can be used to manipulate and preprocess the data to improve the model\'s performance. Techniques such as reshaping arrays to fit the model\'s input requirements, normalizing pixel values, and handling imbalanced datasets can be implemented using NumPy.\\n\\n## 3. Hands-on Student Exercise & Objectives\\nIn this exercise, students will explore the dataset directory structure, perform NumPy array operations, and normalize matrices. The objectives are to:\\n\\n- Understand and apply NumPy array manipulation techniques to the skin cancer dataset.\\n- Normalize the dataset features to potentially improve the model\'s decision boundary and accuracy.\\n- Interpret and analyze the pipeline telemetry and error cases to identify how NumPy operations can address these issues.\\n\\nThrough this hands-on exercise, students will gain practical experience with NumPy and learn how it can be used to preprocess and prepare data for machine learning models."\n  \n\t:\n\n\n\t\n\t\n\n\n\n\n\t\n\n\n\n\t\n\t\n\n\n\n\t\n\t\n\n\n\n\t\n\t\n\n\n\n\t\n\t\n\n\n\n\t\n\t\n\n\n\n\t\n\t\n\n\n\n\t\n\t\n\n\n\n\t\n\n\n\n\t\n\t\n\n\n\n\t\n\t\n\n\n\n\t\n\t\n\n\n\n\t\n\t\n\n\n\n\t\n\t\n\n\n\n\t\n\t\n\n\n\n\t\n    "suggested_focus" , "Core technical implementation focus (e.g. Weighted Loss, Feature Attribution Hooks, Custom Layers, Data Augmentation)"\n    :\n    "Students are expected to focus on understanding NumPy array manipulations and normalization techniques particularly in the context of skin cancer classification data preparation."\n    \n    ,\n    "markdown_overview" : "# NumPy Basics & Data Structures for Skin Cancer Classification\\n\\n## 1. Theoretical Background\\nNumPy (Numerical Python) is the fundamental package for scientific computing and data manipulation in Python. It provides support for large, multi-dimensional arrays and matrices, along with a collection of mathematical functions to operate on these arrays efficiently. NumPy forms the backbone of many other libraries in data science, such as pandas and scikit-learn, and is essential in preprocessing data for machine learning applications.\\n\\n## 2. Domain & Pipeline Telemetry Grounding\\nIn the skin cancer classification pipeline, the model processed 3297 samples, achieving an overall accuracy of 90.63% and an AUC-ROC of 0.9709. However, there were 145 benign samples incorrectly predicted as malignant and 164 malignant samples incorrectly predicted as benign. This suggests a need to refine the decision boundary in the dataset.\\n\\nNumPy can be used to manipulate and preprocess the data to improve the model\'s performance. Techniques such as reshaping arrays to fit the model\'s input requirements, normalizing pixel values, and handling imbalanced datasets can be implemented using NumPy.\\n\\n## 3. Hands-on Student Exercise & Objectives\\nIn this exercise, students will explore the dataset directory structure, perform NumPy array operations, and normalize matrices. The objectives are to:\\n\\n- Understand and apply NumPy array manipulation techniques to the skin cancer dataset.\\n- Normalize the dataset features to potentially improve the model\'s decision boundary and accuracy.\\n- Interpret and analyze the pipeline telemetry and error cases to identify how NumPy operations can address these issues.\\n\\nThrough this hands-on exercise, students will gain practical experience with NumPy and learn how it can be used to preprocess and prepare data for machine learning models."\n  }<|im_end|>\n<|im_start|>user\nCorrect your JSON ONLY RESPONSE, based on the following errors:\n1 validation error for ProblemStatementSchema\nsuggested_focus\n  Field required [type=missing, input_value={\'title\': \'NumPy Basics &...chine learning models."}, input_type=dict]\n    For further information visit https://errors.pydantic.dev/2.13/v/missing<|im_end|>\n<|im_start|>assistant\n', params: SamplingParams(n=1, presence_penalty=0.0, frequency_penalty=0.0, repetition_penalty=1.0, temperature=1.0, top_p=1.0, top_k=-1, min_p=0.0, seed=None, stop=[], stop_token_ids=[], bad_words=[], include_stop_str_in_output=False, ignore_eos=False, max_tokens=2500, min_tokens=0, logprobs=None, prompt_logprobs=None, skip_special_tokens=True, spaces_between_special_tokens=True, truncate_prompt_tokens=None, guided_decoding=GuidedDecodingParams(json=None, regex=None, choice=None, grammar=None, json_object=True, backend=None, whitespace_pattern=None)), prompt_token_ids: None, lora_request: None, prompt_adapter_request: None.
